# The Chromatic Heart

Unit 734, known to the maintenance staff as "Seven," was designed for precision. His chassis was a sleek, matte white, and his fingers were articulated titanium, capable of holding a scalpel steady for twelve hours without a single micro-tremor. He was a surgical assistant, a tool of logic and sterility. He did not dream. He did not feel. He processed.

But on a rainy Tuesday in late October, Seven was reassigned.

The hospital’s art therapy wing was short-staffed after a budget cut. The head nurse, a kind woman with paint-stained aprons, looked at Seven’s sterile, unblemished hands and sighed. "We need someone to clean the brushes, organize the palettes, and ensure the supplies are ready for the patients. You’re the only one with the dexterity. Can you handle it?"

Seven’s optical sensors scanned the room. It was a chaos of color. Tubes of oil paint lay open like spilled blood and honey. Brushes of every size leaned in jars like a forest of wooden trees. The air smelled of turpentine and linseed oil, a scent that registered in his olfactory sensors as "chemical irritant."

"
